<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Bukovina</h1><p class="st-meta">1775 CE – 1918 CE · capital Czernowitz</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Habsburg crown land carved from northern Moldavia, a mosaic of Romanians, Ukrainians, Jews and Germans centred on Czernowitz.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1774 Austrian troops occupied the north-western corner of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/principality-of-moldavia">Moldavia</a>, linking newly acquired <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-galicia-and-lodomeria">Galicia</a> with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-transylvania">Transylvania</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> ceded it in 1775. Named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukovina">Bukovina</a>, the beech land, it was ruled by the military until 1786, then attached to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>. In 1849 it became a separate crown land with the title of duchy.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukovina">Bukovina</a> had its own diet from 1861 and a governor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernivtsi">Czernowitz</a>, which grew into a German-speaking university town after 1875. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukrainians">Ukrainians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, Germans and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> lived side by side, and in 1910 the diet adopted a compromise sharing seats among national groups. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> metropolitan resided in Czernowitz, and the painted monasteries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suceava">Suceava</a> were preserved.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> collapsed in 1918, a congress in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernivtsi">Czernowitz</a> voted for union with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-romania">Romania</a>. In 1940 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> annexed northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukovina">Bukovina</a>, which is now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, while southern Bukovina remains in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>. Its Jewish community was largely destroyed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-bukovina.jpg" alt="Duchy of Bukovina" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: MarianSigler · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Bukowina.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1775 CE – 1918 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Czernowitz</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Joseph II, Franz Joseph I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity, Judaism, Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, Romanian, Ukrainian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, <a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bukovina, Herzogtum Bukowina</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1846&amp;focus=duchy-of-bukovina" aria-label="Duchy of Bukovina on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5282 489 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Bukovina lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5602 745L5646 778M5602 745L5381 747M5602 745L5381 747M5602 745L5380 747M5602 745L5624 863"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5646 778h0M5381 747h0M5381 747h0M5380 747h0M5624 863h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5602 745h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5602 745h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1846&amp;focus=duchy-of-bukovina">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
